WEBVTT

00:00:00.101 --> 00:00:00.493
Welcome.

00:00:00.898 --> 00:00:03.947
This course will take you from the three files
you started with

00:00:03.947 --> 00:00:07.047
to a clear explanation of the repository you now
have.

00:00:07.452 --> 00:00:10.235
You do not need a networking or machine learning
background.

00:00:10.627 --> 00:00:14.486
We will read the paper&#x27;s ideas, inspect both
spreadsheet shaped

00:00:14.486 --> 00:00:19.002
datasets, follow a flow through the original
model, and examine

00:00:19.002 --> 00:00:20.837
the experiments that actually ran.

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:25.252
Then we will walk through the code, setup,
tests, downloads,

00:00:25.454 --> 00:00:27.719
demonstration, and hardware roadmap.

00:00:28.124 --> 00:00:30.363
Codex wrote and produced this lesson.

00:00:30.768 --> 00:00:33.285
A synthetic voice renders the narration.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.423
The research claims come from recorded evidence.

00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:40.885
The central result is a working, measured
reproduction attempt.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.059
The original multimodal classifier trained and
performed

00:00:45.072 --> 00:00:49.802
inference on compatible research flows using an
NVIDIA G B ten

00:00:49.802 --> 00:00:50.978
graphics processor.

00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:55.911
Three full fine tuning runs achieved an average
test accuracy of

00:00:56.037 --> 00:00:57.859
eighty six point six five percent.

00:00:58.263 --> 00:01:02.222
The paper reports ninety seven point five
percent for its

00:01:02.222 --> 00:01:02.842
comparison.

00:01:03.247 --> 00:01:04.891
We did not reproduce that number.

00:01:05.308 --> 00:01:08.989
We also did not build a complete live intrusion
detection system.

00:01:09.406 --> 00:01:11.834
Those limits do not erase the work.

00:01:12.252 --> 00:01:15.679
They tell you precisely what the work
establishes and what a next

00:01:15.679 --> 00:01:17.387
experiment needs to resolve.

00:01:17.843 --> 00:01:20.270
A network moves information in packets.

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:23.293
Think of one packet as a small delivery
envelope.

00:01:23.685 --> 00:01:26.833
Its header contains information used to handle
the delivery.

00:01:27.251 --> 00:01:31.031
Its payload contains the carried content, which
may be encrypted.

00:01:31.436 --> 00:01:35.306
A byte is a small numerical unit with values
from zero to two

00:01:35.306 --> 00:01:36.241
hundred fifty five.

00:01:36.646 --> 00:01:40.048
The model can process those numbers without
turning them into

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.135
readable sentences.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.890
The envelope analogy is only a starting point:
real packets use

00:01:45.902 --> 00:01:49.835
several protocol layers, and a payload is not
guaranteed to

00:01:49.848 --> 00:01:51.997
contain a complete application message.

00:01:52.415 --> 00:01:55.829
One conversation usually spans many packets.

00:01:56.289 --> 00:02:00.274
A flow is a set of related packets, grouped
using an explicit

00:02:00.274 --> 00:02:00.640
rule.

00:02:01.058 --> 00:02:04.815
A common starting point uses source and
destination addresses,

00:02:05.081 --> 00:02:08.206
source and destination ports, and the transport
protocol.

00:02:08.598 --> 00:02:12.317
Direction handling, timeouts, and packet
ordering also need a

00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:12.937
definition.

00:02:13.329 --> 00:02:16.618
Imagine two cameras sending messages at the same
time.

00:02:17.023 --> 00:02:20.654
The next two captured packets could belong to
different cameras.

00:02:21.059 --> 00:02:25.069
Their proximity in a file does not make them one
conversation.

00:02:25.487 --> 00:02:29.231
This matters because Net Mamba Plus learns from
a structured view

00:02:29.231 --> 00:02:29.876
of a flow.

00:02:30.294 --> 00:02:34.874
Supplying unrelated packet rows changes the
meaning of its input.

00:02:35.309 --> 00:02:40.371
An intrusion detection system, often shortened
to I D S, observes

00:02:40.384 --> 00:02:42.978
traffic and raises alerts for investigation.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:46.661
The neural classifier is one component that can
help assign a

00:02:46.674 --> 00:02:47.269
category.

00:02:47.686 --> 00:02:52.571
Capture, flow assembly, data transformation,
alert thresholds,

00:02:52.824 --> 00:02:56.115
event storage, and operational response are
additional

00:02:56.115 --> 00:02:56.811
components.

00:02:57.191 --> 00:03:00.544
A prediction alone does not decide whether to
block a customer

00:03:00.557 --> 00:03:01.076
connection.

00:03:01.455 --> 00:03:05.379
In this project the browser demonstration
displays previously

00:03:05.391 --> 00:03:06.594
recorded predictions.

00:03:07.011 --> 00:03:10.365
That is useful for explaining behavior and
mistakes, but opening

00:03:10.365 --> 00:03:14.516
the page does not connect to a network tap or
execute a model

00:03:14.516 --> 00:03:15.566
inside your browser.

00:03:16.085 --> 00:03:19.171
Throughout the lesson, separate three claims.

00:03:19.563 --> 00:03:22.649
Implemented means there is code intended to do a
job.

00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:26.203
Executed means a recorded command actually ran.

00:03:26.621 --> 00:03:30.010
Validated means a specific check passed against
a stated

00:03:30.023 --> 00:03:30.820
expectation.

00:03:31.212 --> 00:03:34.336
A script can exist without its hardware path
being tested.

00:03:34.728 --> 00:03:38.181
A training job can complete without matching a
published

00:03:38.194 --> 00:03:38.864
accuracy.

00:03:39.256 --> 00:03:43.076
A checksum can match without proving the dataset
is unbiased.

00:03:43.443 --> 00:03:47.578
When someone says everything works, ask which
activity, on which

00:03:47.578 --> 00:03:49.387
environment, with which evidence.

00:03:49.792 --> 00:03:54.446
The repository&#x27;s manifests, logs, predictions,
and failure

00:03:54.446 --> 00:03:56.255
records make that question answerable.

00:03:56.735 --> 00:03:59.619
You now have the vocabulary needed for the rest
of the course.

00:04:00.037 --> 00:04:03.744
We will use short questions to check
understanding, with clearly

00:04:03.744 --> 00:04:06.300
labeled pauses that resume automatically.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:10.412
The transcript repeats the narration and links
each scene to

00:04:10.412 --> 00:04:11.513
supporting files.

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:16.232
The companion file guide explains every tracked
file and every

00:04:16.232 --> 00:04:19.433
release download, so you can look up a
particular filename

00:04:19.522 --> 00:04:21.382
without memorizing the directory tree.

00:04:21.837 --> 00:04:24.469
First, practice the most important boundary.

00:04:24.874 --> 00:04:28.531
Does a working classifier prove that a complete
live security

00:04:28.543 --> 00:04:29.644
system has been deployed?

00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:32.617
Take ten seconds to formulate your answer.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:43.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Does a working
classifier prove a complete live IDS?

00:04:43.101 --> 00:04:46.905
The answer is no: the classifier is one tested
component.

00:04:47.322 --> 00:04:49.634
Now open the attached paper conceptually.

00:04:50.051 --> 00:04:54.423
It is the January twenty twenty six first arXiv
version of Net

00:04:54.423 --> 00:04:58.631
Mamba Plus, a framework of pretrained models for
efficient and

00:04:58.694 --> 00:05:00.627
accurate network traffic classification.

00:05:01.032 --> 00:05:04.380
A research paper describes a problem, a proposed
method,

00:05:04.772 --> 00:05:06.655
experiments, and conclusions.

00:05:07.059 --> 00:05:10.559
It is not an installation manual or a complete
specification of

00:05:10.698 --> 00:05:12.088
every released data file.

00:05:12.505 --> 00:05:15.917
This paper covers several model variants and
datasets.

00:05:16.321 --> 00:05:20.807
Our repository exercises a particular multimodal
Mamba classifier

00:05:21.262 --> 00:05:25.609
and one compatible downstream dataset, so its
scope is narrower.

00:05:26.068 --> 00:05:28.986
The authors identify three difficulties.

00:05:29.429 --> 00:05:33.775
First, some sequence models become expensive
when processing many

00:05:33.838 --> 00:05:34.799
input units.

00:05:35.203 --> 00:05:39.929
Second, a traffic representation may discard
useful content while

00:05:39.929 --> 00:05:43.795
keeping misleading clues, such as identifiers
tied to the

00:05:43.795 --> 00:05:44.831
collection environment.

00:05:45.299 --> 00:05:49.152
Third, common categories can dominate learning
while rare

00:05:49.152 --> 00:05:50.757
categories perform poorly.

00:05:51.174 --> 00:05:52.539
These are distinct problems.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:57.100
A faster model does not automatically fix biased
input data.

00:05:57.517 --> 00:06:01.661
Adding packet timing does not automatically
solve a rare class

00:06:01.661 --> 00:06:02.129
problem.

00:06:02.558 --> 00:06:06.576
The paper combines architectural,
representation, and training

00:06:06.576 --> 00:06:08.042
ideas to address them.

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:11.744
Our measurements should be interpreted against
the parts actually

00:06:11.757 --> 00:06:12.300
enabled.

00:06:12.768 --> 00:06:16.522
Multimodal means using several views of the same
observation.

00:06:16.939 --> 00:06:21.047
Here it means packet byte content, packet
lengths, and time gaps.

00:06:21.451 --> 00:06:25.635
Suppose two flows contain similarly encrypted
bytes, but one

00:06:25.635 --> 00:06:29.389
sends a steady series of small packets while
another sends large

00:06:29.402 --> 00:06:29.983
bursts.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:33.611
Size and timing can provide additional
structure.

00:06:34.028 --> 00:06:37.668
This is an explanatory example, not a measured
claim about our

00:06:37.681 --> 00:06:38.439
test set.

00:06:38.856 --> 00:06:42.496
The important requirement is that all three
views describe the

00:06:42.496 --> 00:06:43.381
same flow.

00:06:43.773 --> 00:06:46.731
Combining bytes from one connection with timing
from another

00:06:46.731 --> 00:06:50.750
would create a physically inconsistent example
even if the tensor

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:52.115
dimensions still matched.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:56.208
The paper discusses Mamba and an alternative
optimized

00:06:56.208 --> 00:06:58.116
Transformer called Net Trans.

00:06:58.218 --> 00:07:01.238
Flash Attention belongs to that alternative&#x27;s
attention

00:07:01.238 --> 00:07:04.209
implementation; it is not evidence that our
active Mamba

00:07:04.221 --> 00:07:06.420
classifier uses an attention layer.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:10.162
A Mamba block processes a sequence while
updating numerical

00:07:10.162 --> 00:07:13.182
state, with updates influenced by the input.

00:07:13.587 --> 00:07:15.799
This can make sequence processing efficient.

00:07:16.203 --> 00:07:20.134
It does not mean the software maintains a live
table of network

00:07:20.147 --> 00:07:21.853
connections between predictions.

00:07:22.257 --> 00:07:26.163
In our tested configuration the classifier uses
four Mamba

00:07:26.163 --> 00:07:26.744
blocks.

00:07:27.149 --> 00:07:30.347
Other branches present in a source repository
are not

00:07:30.435 --> 00:07:33.216
automatically exercised by that configuration.

00:07:33.739 --> 00:07:37.178
For a first reading, use the framework diagram
to understand the

00:07:37.191 --> 00:07:38.202
overall path.

00:07:38.606 --> 00:07:41.982
Then read the traffic representation section to
see how raw

00:07:41.982 --> 00:07:44.334
traffic becomes structured inputs.

00:07:44.751 --> 00:07:48.329
The model details explain embeddings, sequence
processing,

00:07:48.594 --> 00:07:50.617
pretraining, and classification.

00:07:51.022 --> 00:07:54.903
The online system section adds capture and
shared memory around a

00:07:54.916 --> 00:07:55.725
classifier.

00:07:56.129 --> 00:08:00.352
The evaluation section reports dataset
comparisons, efficiency,

00:08:00.605 --> 00:08:01.983
and other experiments.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.977
Keep a note beside each number identifying the
model, task, and

00:08:06.977 --> 00:08:08.165
machine that produced it.

00:08:08.582 --> 00:08:11.857
This reading habit prevents a result from one
experiment being

00:08:11.933 --> 00:08:15.308
accidentally used as proof for a different
system.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:19.686
The paper&#x27;s comparison includes a ninety seven
point five percent

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:25.299
accuracy figure for the relevant C I C IoT
twenty twenty two

00:08:25.299 --> 00:08:25.754
result.

00:08:26.158 --> 00:08:29.698
Our three seed mean is eighty six point six five
percent.

00:08:30.102 --> 00:08:33.945
We preserve both numbers and the differences in
runtime and

00:08:33.945 --> 00:08:34.956
training settings.

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.611
We did not isolate the cause of the gap.

00:08:38.015 --> 00:08:41.782
The paper also studies few shot learning,
unfamiliar

00:08:41.782 --> 00:08:45.410
distributions, and a label distribution aware
strategy for

00:08:45.422 --> 00:08:46.560
imbalanced classes.

00:08:46.977 --> 00:08:51.136
Those results do not become reproduced merely
because our basic

00:08:51.136 --> 00:08:52.425
classifier runs.

00:08:52.842 --> 00:08:55.965
Each additional claim would need its own
matching protocol,

00:08:56.394 --> 00:08:58.227
inputs, and measured outputs.

00:08:58.701 --> 00:09:00.976
Two details deserve special care.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.298
A token is a numerical input unit for the
sequence model; we will

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:06.663
build those units later.

00:09:07.093 --> 00:09:10.998
The paper&#x27;s hyperparameter table shows four
hundred one tokens

00:09:11.099 --> 00:09:12.768
for its byte representation.

00:09:13.172 --> 00:09:17.318
Our actual multimodal classifier receives four
hundred forty

00:09:17.318 --> 00:09:21.792
three tokens, after including sizes, intervals,
and three summary

00:09:21.792 --> 00:09:22.398
tokens.

00:09:22.803 --> 00:09:27.049
Later, the paper reports online throughput of
two hundred sixty

00:09:27.062 --> 00:09:31.055
one point eight seven megabits per second for
its Net Mamba

00:09:31.068 --> 00:09:32.774
prototype on different hardware.

00:09:33.179 --> 00:09:35.820
That is not our measured multimodal deployment.

00:09:36.225 --> 00:09:40.484
Before presenting any paper number, identify the
exact experiment

00:09:40.484 --> 00:09:41.356
it describes.

00:09:41.773 --> 00:09:46.752
Take ten seconds to name one paper claim that
this repository has

00:09:46.752 --> 00:09:48.636
not independently reproduced.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:59.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Name one paper
claim that has not been reproduced here.

00:09:59.101 --> 00:10:02.560
Examples include the paper&#x27;s headline accuracy,
its full

00:10:02.572 --> 00:10:05.752
pretraining protocol, and its online throughput.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:08.932
Next, inspect your first attached data file.

00:10:09.337 --> 00:10:13.797
A C S V is plain text organized as rows and
comma separated

00:10:13.809 --> 00:10:14.430
fields.

00:10:14.823 --> 00:10:18.167
A spreadsheet can display it as a table, but
this file is not a

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.752
database server or a network capture program.

00:10:21.157 --> 00:10:24.717
The full profile counted one million four
hundred ten thousand

00:10:24.881 --> 00:10:29.480
two hundred fifty five packet records in the C I
C I D S

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.000
twenty seventeen file.

00:10:31.406 --> 00:10:33.585
Each row has fifteen hundred five columns.

00:10:34.003 --> 00:10:37.246
The stored profile lets you inspect that
structure without

00:10:37.258 --> 00:10:39.665
opening a multi gigabyte spreadsheet.

00:10:40.185 --> 00:10:43.557
Fifteen hundred columns are named payload byte
one through

00:10:43.557 --> 00:10:47.309
payload byte fifteen hundred, using underscores
in the exact

00:10:47.309 --> 00:10:48.019
header names.

00:10:48.438 --> 00:10:50.973
They describe payload byte positions.

00:10:51.429 --> 00:10:55.688
Five additional columns are time to live, total
length, protocol,

00:10:55.967 --> 00:10:58.109
a delta time field, and the label.

00:10:58.515 --> 00:11:02.584
Time to live is a protocol field limiting how
far a packet can

00:11:02.597 --> 00:11:04.625
travel; it is not a timestamp.

00:11:05.043 --> 00:11:07.730
Total length is a recorded packet length value.

00:11:08.136 --> 00:11:10.874
Protocol describes the communication protocol.

00:11:11.292 --> 00:11:15.754
Delta time suggests a time difference, but the
CSV alone does not

00:11:15.754 --> 00:11:18.366
establish its units or how it was computed.

00:11:18.784 --> 00:11:21.319
The label is the category supplied with the
record.

00:11:21.801 --> 00:11:24.843
This CSV contains fifteen label names.

00:11:25.249 --> 00:11:29.470
The largest group is labeled benign, with three
hundred sixty two

00:11:29.622 --> 00:11:31.472
thousand one hundred eight rows.

00:11:31.878 --> 00:11:35.883
Do S Hulk has two hundred fifty thousand, and D
Do S has two

00:11:35.883 --> 00:11:38.190
hundred forty one thousand four hundred five.

00:11:38.595 --> 00:11:42.347
In general terms, a denial of service attack
attempts to disrupt

00:11:42.347 --> 00:11:46.263
availability, and a distributed denial of
service uses multiple

00:11:46.263 --> 00:11:46.948
sources.

00:11:47.391 --> 00:11:50.953
Here those are dataset labels, not events newly
detected by our

00:11:50.953 --> 00:11:51.396
model.

00:11:51.827 --> 00:11:56.048
The table also contains credential attack, web
attack, scanning,

00:11:56.302 --> 00:11:59.369
bot, infiltration, and other categories.

00:11:59.774 --> 00:12:04.730
Their counts describe this supplied extract, not
every version of

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:05.858
the original dataset.

00:12:06.335 --> 00:12:09.932
At the small end, Port Scan has eight hundred
thirty rows.

00:12:10.363 --> 00:12:13.581
The web attack SQL injection label has only
twelve.

00:12:13.999 --> 00:12:17.267
That is why a single overall accuracy can hide a
serious

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:17.901
weakness.

00:12:18.294 --> 00:12:22.347
Imagine a model that handles a common label well
but fails every

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:23.196
rare example.

00:12:23.589 --> 00:12:26.224
Its aggregate score might still look impressive.

00:12:26.604 --> 00:12:30.151
A defensible packet classifier study would
examine per class

00:12:30.164 --> 00:12:34.547
support, precision, recall, and a confusion
matrix, with careful

00:12:34.547 --> 00:12:34.990
splitting.

00:12:35.396 --> 00:12:37.930
We have not trained that packet classifier here.

00:12:38.335 --> 00:12:41.679
These counts explain a possible future
adaptation and its

00:12:41.692 --> 00:12:45.176
evaluation requirements; they do not report new
model

00:12:45.189 --> 00:12:45.987
performance.

00:12:46.468 --> 00:12:51.131
The full metadata scan also found negative delta
times, with a

00:12:51.144 --> 00:12:55.668
minimum of negative zero point zero zero zero
zero one eight.

00:12:56.073 --> 00:12:58.126
We do not know the time unit from this extract.

00:12:58.544 --> 00:13:02.764
Some recorded total lengths exceed fifteen
hundred even though

00:13:02.777 --> 00:13:04.677
there are fifteen hundred payload columns.

00:13:05.083 --> 00:13:08.504
Those observations are reasons to investigate
the extraction

00:13:08.504 --> 00:13:09.201
procedure.

00:13:09.619 --> 00:13:13.320
They are not enough to diagnose corruption,
reconstruct missing

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:15.423
headers, or invent time ordering.

00:13:15.816 --> 00:13:19.301
A field name is a clue, not a complete data
contract.

00:13:19.694 --> 00:13:23.330
Our strict native flow path requires observed
intervals to be

00:13:23.343 --> 00:13:27.512
finite and nonnegative, so blindly copying these
values would be

00:13:27.525 --> 00:13:28.957
unsafe scientifically.

00:13:29.435 --> 00:13:33.745
The profiling program scans the full file to
count rows and

00:13:33.758 --> 00:13:35.685
aggregate the selected metadata.

00:13:36.103 --> 00:13:39.564
That is stronger evidence than looking at the
first fifty rows.

00:13:39.995 --> 00:13:43.824
However, it is narrower than exhaustively
validating every

00:13:43.836 --> 00:13:45.104
payload byte cell.

00:13:45.522 --> 00:13:48.400
The report states that limit explicitly.

00:13:48.793 --> 00:13:53.116
It records file size and a content hash so
another person can

00:13:53.116 --> 00:13:54.612
identify the same input.

00:13:55.030 --> 00:13:59.150
It does not recover capture history, packet
direction, or a flow

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:01.496
identifier that the columns do not supply.

00:14:01.889 --> 00:14:06.224
Keeping the exact scope of a data audit visible
prevents a useful

00:14:06.237 --> 00:14:09.571
profile from being mistaken for a complete
certification.

00:14:10.022 --> 00:14:13.902
Most importantly, the supplied schema does not
establish source

00:14:13.902 --> 00:14:18.453
and destination addresses, ports, a flow
identifier, or packet

00:14:18.453 --> 00:14:19.429
order within a flow.

00:14:19.835 --> 00:14:23.841
Its payload byte columns also do not establish
the paper&#x27;s header

00:14:24.133 --> 00:14:25.553
and payload representation.

00:14:25.958 --> 00:14:30.206
Taking five neighboring rows and calling them
one flow would add

00:14:30.218 --> 00:14:31.524
an unsupported assumption.

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:35.835
A classifier trained directly on these rows
could still be an

00:14:35.847 --> 00:14:39.220
interesting project, but it would be a packet
based adaptation

00:14:39.232 --> 00:14:41.261
with its own labels and evaluation.

00:14:41.666 --> 00:14:45.280
Take ten seconds: explain why matching the
number of numeric

00:14:45.292 --> 00:14:48.982
columns would not make these rows compatible Net
Mamba Plus

00:14:48.994 --> 00:14:49.628
inputs.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:15:00.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Why is matching a
tensor shape insufficient to establish
compatible flow data?

00:15:00.089 --> 00:15:04.347
A tensor shape describes dimensions, not the
meaning or origin of

00:15:04.347 --> 00:15:05.006
the numbers.

00:15:05.411 --> 00:15:08.326
Your second CSV makes that distinction clearer.

00:15:08.744 --> 00:15:13.534
The U N S W extract contains seventy nine
thousand eight hundred

00:15:13.686 --> 00:15:14.764
eighty one rows.

00:15:15.169 --> 00:15:18.768
It has the same broad structure: fifteen hundred
payload byte

00:15:18.768 --> 00:15:23.216
columns followed by time to live, total length,
protocol, delta

00:15:23.216 --> 00:15:24.382
time, and label.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:26.549
There are ten category names.

00:15:26.942 --> 00:15:30.212
The common column layout makes joint profiling
convenient.

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:34.432
It does not prove the two files were captured,
labeled, or

00:15:34.444 --> 00:15:37.638
extracted with the same procedure, and it does
not make their

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:39.349
category names interchangeable.

00:15:39.835 --> 00:15:44.713
The largest U N S W category is normal, with
twenty one thousand

00:15:44.713 --> 00:15:45.220
rows.

00:15:45.638 --> 00:15:49.731
Generic contains seventeen thousand five hundred
eighty, and

00:15:49.744 --> 00:15:53.583
exploits contains thirteen thousand nine hundred
ninety two.

00:15:54.027 --> 00:15:57.398
Fuzzers and reconnaissance are also substantial
groups.

00:15:57.816 --> 00:16:01.199
At a high level, reconnaissance means attempts
to learn about a

00:16:01.199 --> 00:16:05.812
target, and fuzzing probes how software handles
unusual inputs.

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:09.829
These descriptions help interpret the names, but
the exact

00:16:09.841 --> 00:16:12.743
labeling rules come from the dataset&#x27;s
production process.

00:16:13.174 --> 00:16:15.277
The table is a count of stored labels.

00:16:15.683 --> 00:16:19.294
It is not a claim that our trained six class
model recognizes

00:16:19.307 --> 00:16:20.790
these ten categories.

00:16:21.281 --> 00:16:26.404
The remaining labels are do s, backdoor,
analysis, shellcode, and

00:16:26.404 --> 00:16:26.975
worms.

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:29.562
Worms has only ninety three rows.

00:16:29.955 --> 00:16:34.051
Unlike the mostly T C P and U D P protocol
entries in the

00:16:34.051 --> 00:16:37.969
first extract, this file includes a broader
range of protocol

00:16:37.982 --> 00:16:38.552
strings.

00:16:38.971 --> 00:16:42.357
Some may be uncommon to a beginner, but the
practical lesson is

00:16:42.369 --> 00:16:46.643
simple: a future preprocessing program must
handle the actual

00:16:46.643 --> 00:16:49.585
declared values rather than assume two
protocols.

00:16:49.991 --> 00:16:52.628
The full profile records their counts by label.

00:16:53.034 --> 00:16:57.460
Again, no packet model was trained on this
extract, and these

00:16:57.460 --> 00:17:01.340
category frequencies do not describe the native
flow dataset used

00:17:01.353 --> 00:17:02.152
in our experiment.

00:17:02.635 --> 00:17:06.274
All three data sources concern network traffic,
but they define

00:17:06.286 --> 00:17:07.529
different learning problems.

00:17:07.947 --> 00:17:12.804
Your C I C I D S extract has fifteen packet
label names.

00:17:13.222 --> 00:17:15.859
Your U N S W extract has ten.

00:17:16.265 --> 00:17:21.375
The authors&#x27; compatible C I C IoT twenty twenty
two flow release

00:17:21.578 --> 00:17:23.048
has six class meanings.

00:17:23.441 --> 00:17:27.626
A classifier&#x27;s output layer is tied to its
training labels.

00:17:28.044 --> 00:17:31.949
Six output numbers cannot simply be relabeled
with a new ten

00:17:31.962 --> 00:17:33.293
category vocabulary.

00:17:33.712 --> 00:17:36.615
Changing the dataset can require a new class
mapping,

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:40.698
classification head, training protocol, and
evaluation.

00:17:41.104 --> 00:17:44.857
The repository checks that a saved classifier
stays attached to

00:17:44.857 --> 00:17:46.378
the correct class meanings.

00:17:46.868 --> 00:17:50.872
To exercise the original Net Mamba Plus path, we
acquired the

00:17:50.936 --> 00:17:55.282
authors&#x27; native flow release rather than
silently fabricate flow

00:17:55.282 --> 00:17:57.157
context for the CSV rows.

00:17:57.563 --> 00:18:00.756
The training split has eight thousand three
hundred twenty three

00:18:00.756 --> 00:18:05.216
flows, validation has one thousand forty, and
the test split has

00:18:05.216 --> 00:18:06.559
one thousand forty one.

00:18:06.977 --> 00:18:11.260
Those three files total ten thousand four
hundred four flows.

00:18:11.679 --> 00:18:13.757
The provided split boundaries were retained.

00:18:14.175 --> 00:18:18.027
This choice makes the original loader and
classifier executable

00:18:18.217 --> 00:18:19.547
with meaningful inputs.

00:18:19.953 --> 00:18:23.729
It also means the actual training result is not
a result on

00:18:23.894 --> 00:18:25.655
either of your uploaded CSVs.

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:28.988
That distinction belongs in the customer
presentation.

00:18:29.455 --> 00:18:33.538
Compatibility is not a guarantee of independent
evaluation.

00:18:33.944 --> 00:18:37.773
The validation report found five identical
stored inputs shared

00:18:37.773 --> 00:18:41.755
between training and validation, and six shared
between training

00:18:41.869 --> 00:18:42.541
and test.

00:18:42.997 --> 00:18:47.778
File names and hashes identify records, but they
cannot establish

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:49.959
that all physical captures are independent.

00:18:50.364 --> 00:18:53.991
The released pretrained checkpoint&#x27;s earlier
exposure is also

00:18:54.067 --> 00:18:54.574
unknown.

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:58.746
We preserved the authors&#x27; splits and disclosed
these issues.

00:18:59.139 --> 00:19:03.653
A stronger future generalization study would
define a new capture

00:19:03.742 --> 00:19:06.582
independent holdout and document its provenance.

00:19:06.988 --> 00:19:10.145
Repeating inference on the existing test file
checks

00:19:10.196 --> 00:19:14.228
reproducibility of that result; it does not
create a new holdout.

00:19:14.701 --> 00:19:17.959
This is the connection between your original
three files and the

00:19:17.959 --> 00:19:18.771
repository.

00:19:19.176 --> 00:19:21.661
The PDF provides the research method.

00:19:22.054 --> 00:19:26.110
The CSVs provide packet records that can be
profiled, but do not

00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:28.696
establish the method&#x27;s required flow context.

00:19:29.114 --> 00:19:33.107
The repository therefore preserves that finding
and runs a

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:35.744
compatible native flow experiment instead.

00:19:36.162 --> 00:19:39.813
If you later obtain raw captures with
trustworthy grouping and

00:19:39.813 --> 00:19:43.781
extraction rules, you can investigate a matching
data preparation

00:19:43.781 --> 00:19:44.288
path.

00:19:44.694 --> 00:19:48.522
Alternatively, you can explicitly design a
packet classifier

00:19:48.522 --> 00:19:49.219
adaptation.

00:19:49.637 --> 00:19:53.681
Take ten seconds to explain which data was
actually used for the

00:19:53.694 --> 00:19:55.633
published training runs, and why.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:06.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Which data was
actually used for training, and why were the
uploaded CSVs not used?

00:20:06.101 --> 00:20:09.883
The actual experiment uses the authors&#x27; native
flow files.

00:20:10.301 --> 00:20:13.324
Each file contains a JSON array of flow objects.

00:20:13.742 --> 00:20:17.271
JSON is structured text with named fields and
lists.

00:20:17.688 --> 00:20:20.547
The data field holds a list of packet byte
strings.

00:20:20.939 --> 00:20:24.772
Sizes and intervals are space separated numeric
strings.

00:20:25.177 --> 00:20:28.061
Labeled examples also carry class information.

00:20:28.466 --> 00:20:31.464
The small example on screen is synthetic and
deliberately

00:20:31.476 --> 00:20:33.424
shortened to explain the schema.

00:20:33.829 --> 00:20:35.613
It is not a recovered packet capture.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:39.155
The real loader converts these fields into fixed
numerical

00:20:39.167 --> 00:20:43.380
arrays, so variable length flows can enter the
same neural model.

00:20:43.868 --> 00:20:47.765
The byte branch uses the first five packets and
up to three

00:20:47.777 --> 00:20:49.675
hundred twenty stored bytes from each.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:53.154
Five times three hundred twenty gives sixteen
hundred byte

00:20:53.167 --> 00:20:53.888
positions.

00:20:54.293 --> 00:20:58.278
Short inputs receive padding and long inputs are
truncated.

00:20:58.683 --> 00:21:02.339
The paper describes an eighty byte header
allocation and a two

00:21:02.339 --> 00:21:05.021
hundred forty byte payload allocation per
packet.

00:21:05.426 --> 00:21:08.412
The released stored bytes can be loaded, but
their complete

00:21:08.412 --> 00:21:12.068
extraction history has not been independently
verified.

00:21:12.473 --> 00:21:16.180
We therefore say stored bytes when describing
what our executed

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:17.761
loader actually consumed.

00:21:18.179 --> 00:21:21.000
This preserves the difference between a paper&#x27;s
intended

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:24.479
representation and a release&#x27;s established
provenance.

00:21:24.968 --> 00:21:28.281
The loader scales a byte value by dividing by
two hundred fifty

00:21:28.281 --> 00:21:28.800
five.

00:21:29.217 --> 00:21:32.290
It then subtracts one half and divides by one
half.

00:21:32.695 --> 00:21:36.489
Zero becomes negative one, and two hundred fifty
five becomes

00:21:36.489 --> 00:21:37.323
positive one.

00:21:37.728 --> 00:21:40.548
This puts byte inputs on a common numerical
scale.

00:21:40.966 --> 00:21:44.355
A tensor is simply a multidimensional array of
numbers.

00:21:44.810 --> 00:21:49.135
For a batch of flows, the byte tensor has
dimensions batch, one,

00:21:49.679 --> 00:21:51.222
one, and sixteen hundred.

00:21:51.627 --> 00:21:55.205
The extra dimensions match the original model&#x27;s
interface.

00:21:55.572 --> 00:21:59.391
A matching shape is necessary for execution, but
it still does

00:21:59.404 --> 00:22:02.502
not prove that the underlying bytes mean the
right thing.

00:22:02.981 --> 00:22:06.598
The size branch uses the first twenty packet
sizes, clipped

00:22:06.598 --> 00:22:08.811
between zero and fifteen hundred.

00:22:09.216 --> 00:22:11.872
Its padding value is clipped to the same upper
limit.

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:16.034
The timing branch uses the first twenty gaps and
maps an observed

00:22:16.034 --> 00:22:20.182
gap x to one plus x divided by two plus x.

00:22:20.688 --> 00:22:24.432
Zero maps to one half; one maps to two thirds.

00:22:24.837 --> 00:22:28.442
Those are numerical examples, not an assertion
about seconds.

00:22:28.859 --> 00:22:31.680
Missing intervals use padding that maps to one.

00:22:32.072 --> 00:22:35.816
The wrapper requires actual observed gaps to be
finite and

00:22:35.828 --> 00:22:36.524
nonnegative.

00:22:36.916 --> 00:22:40.938
Training and inference must use the same rules
or they present

00:22:40.951 --> 00:22:42.405
different problems to the model.

00:22:42.868 --> 00:22:46.218
A token here is one input unit for the sequence
model.

00:22:46.623 --> 00:22:48.342
It is not a word from a language model.

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:52.034
The sixteen hundred byte positions are grouped
into four byte

00:22:52.034 --> 00:22:55.018
strides, producing four hundred byte tokens.

00:22:55.410 --> 00:23:00.252
Twenty size tokens and twenty interval tokens
add forty more.

00:23:00.695 --> 00:23:04.172
Three learned summary tokens bring the total to
four hundred

00:23:04.172 --> 00:23:04.968
forty three.

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:09.494
Each token is embedded into a vector with two
hundred fifty six

00:23:09.494 --> 00:23:10.164
coordinates.

00:23:10.556 --> 00:23:13.376
An embedding is a learned numerical
representation.

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:17.143
The model learns how useful patterns in those
coordinates relate

00:23:17.156 --> 00:23:18.648
to the dataset categories.

00:23:19.122 --> 00:23:22.056
The sequence passes through four Mamba blocks.

00:23:22.460 --> 00:23:26.342
The active configuration uses the unidirectional
branch and its

00:23:26.342 --> 00:23:28.125
fast execution path.

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:32.134
After processing, the three summary vectors are
added together

00:23:32.539 --> 00:23:34.473
and passed to the classification head.

00:23:34.878 --> 00:23:39.519
The result is six logits, or raw category
scores, for each flow.

00:23:39.936 --> 00:23:44.602
The six class meanings are Flood, R T S P brute
force, Audio,

00:23:44.893 --> 00:23:47.182
Other, Cameras, and Home Automation.

00:23:47.625 --> 00:23:51.406
Four describe device or activity groups, so
these are not six

00:23:51.406 --> 00:23:52.304
attack types.

00:23:52.721 --> 00:23:56.262
The complete classifier has one million eight
hundred seventy

00:23:56.274 --> 00:23:57.994
thousand eighty parameters.

00:23:58.411 --> 00:24:01.497
Parameters are the learned numbers updated
during training.

00:24:01.901 --> 00:24:04.165
They are different from the input tensor values.

00:24:04.570 --> 00:24:08.477
The runtime checks exercise this complete model
rather than

00:24:08.477 --> 00:24:10.614
replacing it with a smaller lookalike.

00:24:11.068 --> 00:24:14.115
To explain the input contract, cover three
levels.

00:24:14.507 --> 00:24:16.719
The fields must describe the correct flow.

00:24:17.124 --> 00:24:20.816
Their dimensions and transformations must match
the original

00:24:20.816 --> 00:24:21.233
loader.

00:24:21.688 --> 00:24:26.075
Finally, the label vocabulary must match the
saved classifier.

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:30.462
A file can pass a basic JSON parser and still
fail one of these

00:24:30.462 --> 00:24:31.233
requirements.

00:24:31.638 --> 00:24:36.430
Our wrappers reject incompatible or invalid
inputs before model

00:24:36.430 --> 00:24:39.009
execution where those conditions can be checked.

00:24:39.426 --> 00:24:43.737
Take ten seconds to describe the three feature
views and the six

00:24:43.737 --> 00:24:47.568
number output, without using the word artificial
intelligence as

00:24:47.568 --> 00:24:49.641
a substitute for the actual process.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:25:00.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Describe the three
feature views and the six-number output.

00:25:00.114 --> 00:25:04.699
Bytes, sizes, and intervals enter the model; six
raw scores come

00:25:04.699 --> 00:25:05.015
out.

00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:08.283
Now separate learning from using what was
learned.

00:25:08.701 --> 00:25:12.855
Pretraining teaches a representation from a
reconstruction task.

00:25:13.311 --> 00:25:17.705
Fine tuning adapts that representation to known
categories using

00:25:17.705 --> 00:25:18.693
labeled examples.

00:25:19.098 --> 00:25:22.568
Inference uses a fixed classifier to produce
scores without

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:24.012
updating its parameters.

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:28.153
A checkpoint saves model state so a later
process can reload it.

00:25:28.559 --> 00:25:32.067
Some training checkpoints also include optimizer
state and

00:25:32.067 --> 00:25:32.738
settings.

00:25:33.156 --> 00:25:36.765
The word checkpoint therefore describes a file&#x27;s
role, not a

00:25:36.765 --> 00:25:39.628
guarantee that it contains a complete deployment
package.

00:25:40.101 --> 00:25:43.940
During masked pretraining, parts of the input
are hidden or

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:46.500
zeroed according to the original procedure.

00:25:46.892 --> 00:25:50.795
A reconstruction decoder tries to recover the
missing content and

00:25:50.795 --> 00:25:51.821
feature values.

00:25:52.239 --> 00:25:54.253
The loss measures reconstruction error.

00:25:54.659 --> 00:25:58.156
The optimizer adjusts parameters to reduce that
error over

00:25:58.156 --> 00:25:58.840
examples.

00:25:59.245 --> 00:26:02.996
Labels such as Flood or Cameras are not the
reconstruction

00:26:02.996 --> 00:26:03.452
target.

00:26:03.857 --> 00:26:07.291
Learning a useful representation this way may
help a later

00:26:07.303 --> 00:26:11.307
classification task, but a falling
reconstruction loss does not

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:13.854
itself prove attack detection accuracy.

00:26:14.272 --> 00:26:18.301
The full paper uses a much larger pretraining
protocol than the

00:26:18.301 --> 00:26:20.214
short functional check we executed.

00:26:20.689 --> 00:26:24.364
Our limited check completed two epochs and one
hundred thirty two

00:26:24.478 --> 00:26:27.127
optimizer updates on downstream training data.

00:26:27.533 --> 00:26:31.690
Reconstruction loss fell from about nine point
five two to six

00:26:31.690 --> 00:26:32.704
point one three.

00:26:33.109 --> 00:26:36.734
The requested short step budget was rounded by
the original loop

00:26:36.937 --> 00:26:38.103
to complete epochs.

00:26:38.496 --> 00:26:41.867
This demonstrates that the masked training path
executes and

00:26:41.867 --> 00:26:43.033
learns on that check.

00:26:43.438 --> 00:26:46.848
It does not recreate the paper&#x27;s one hundred
fifty thousand step

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:47.583
pretraining.

00:26:47.988 --> 00:26:51.119
The three main fine tuning runs started from the
authors&#x27;

00:26:51.132 --> 00:26:54.592
released pretrained checkpoint, not the output
of our short

00:26:54.592 --> 00:26:55.022
check.

00:26:55.428 --> 00:26:58.913
Keep those two branches separate when drawing
the workflow.

00:26:59.368 --> 00:27:03.322
In fine tuning, a batch contains multiple
labeled flows.

00:27:03.753 --> 00:27:06.109
The forward pass computes category scores.

00:27:06.515 --> 00:27:10.291
A classification loss compares those scores with
the supplied

00:27:10.291 --> 00:27:12.496
labels, with the configured smoothing.

00:27:12.902 --> 00:27:16.310
The backward pass computes gradients, which
describe how

00:27:16.323 --> 00:27:18.566
parameter changes affect the loss.

00:27:18.984 --> 00:27:21.671
The optimizer then updates those parameters.

00:27:22.089 --> 00:27:26.017
Repeating this process across the training
loader forms an epoch.

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:29.172
A learning rate controls the scale of updates.

00:27:29.578 --> 00:27:33.658
This is why training needs much more computation
than simply

00:27:33.671 --> 00:27:35.115
opening the recorded demo.

00:27:35.521 --> 00:27:39.956
The demo reads saved outputs; a training command
repeatedly

00:27:39.969 --> 00:27:40.945
changes the model.

00:27:41.435 --> 00:27:43.982
The training split supplies parameter updates.

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:46.794
Validation results select a checkpoint.

00:27:47.225 --> 00:27:50.672
The test split measures the selected
classifier&#x27;s performance

00:27:50.684 --> 00:27:51.166
afterward.

00:27:51.571 --> 00:27:54.726
Choosing a checkpoint by its test score would
use the test set as

00:27:54.726 --> 00:27:55.575
a tuning signal.

00:27:55.993 --> 00:27:59.896
The native fine tuner selects the best
validation accuracy and

00:27:59.896 --> 00:28:02.050
then performs its final test pass.

00:28:02.468 --> 00:28:07.118
Separate evaluation strictly reloads that
selected classifier.

00:28:07.536 --> 00:28:11.680
This separation is necessary, although the
previously disclosed

00:28:11.794 --> 00:28:16.355
input overlaps and unknown capture history still
limit the

00:28:16.368 --> 00:28:16.964
evidence.

00:28:17.407 --> 00:28:22.095
Following the split procedure cannot undo
uncertainty in how the

00:28:22.171 --> 00:28:24.085
underlying data was collected.

00:28:24.555 --> 00:28:28.571
Each of the three declared seeds completed one
hundred twenty

00:28:28.571 --> 00:28:32.055
epochs and seven thousand nine hundred twenty
updates.

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:36.514
A seed sets the starting values for randomized
operations; the

00:28:36.514 --> 00:28:38.996
seeds are not three independent datasets.

00:28:39.402 --> 00:28:43.329
The source based recipe uses batch size one
hundred twenty eight

00:28:43.607 --> 00:28:46.964
and a base learning rate that resolves to an
effective rate of

00:28:46.964 --> 00:28:48.877
zero point zero zero one.

00:28:49.283 --> 00:28:52.829
The paper describes different batch and runtime
conditions.

00:28:53.235 --> 00:28:57.263
Our training ran without mixed precision, while
native evaluation

00:28:57.580 --> 00:28:59.911
used its recorded mixed precision path.

00:29:00.316 --> 00:29:03.888
These details help another engineer reproduce
our execution

00:29:04.218 --> 00:29:07.714
rather than accidentally claim an exact paper
configuration.

00:29:08.201 --> 00:29:12.675
Strict reloading rejects missing or unexpected
model tensors

00:29:12.979 --> 00:29:14.804
rather than quietly ignoring them.

00:29:15.210 --> 00:29:18.657
The wrapper also checks the class meaning
associated with each

00:29:18.657 --> 00:29:19.645
output index.

00:29:20.063 --> 00:29:23.168
This matters because a numerically valid
classifier could

00:29:23.181 --> 00:29:25.906
otherwise display the wrong category names.

00:29:26.311 --> 00:29:30.785
Inference can return logits, display scores, and
a selected class

00:29:30.797 --> 00:29:32.267
for unlabeled flows.

00:29:32.686 --> 00:29:36.462
It cannot calculate accuracy without ground
truth.

00:29:36.868 --> 00:29:40.784
Take ten seconds: explain the difference between
demonstrating a

00:29:40.796 --> 00:29:44.763
short pretraining update, completing fine
tuning, and using a

00:29:44.763 --> 00:29:46.461
saved classifier to predict.

00:29:46.854 --> 00:29:49.629
Name the separate output produced by each
activity.

00:29:50.000 --> 00:30:00.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] What does each of
pretraining, fine-tuning and inference produce?

00:30:00.114 --> 00:30:04.003
Pretraining produces representation weights,
fine tuning produces

00:30:04.016 --> 00:30:08.082
a task specific classifier, and inference
produces predictions.

00:30:08.487 --> 00:30:11.692
Now follow the code that organizes those
activities.

00:30:12.110 --> 00:30:16.227
This repository is an execution and evidence
layer around the

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:17.316
authors&#x27; source.

00:30:17.734 --> 00:30:20.318
It does not claim authorship of Net Mamba Plus.

00:30:20.724 --> 00:30:24.081
The source is fetched at a pinned commit, with a
recorded

00:30:24.093 --> 00:30:27.931
inventory of one hundred three source files and
additional core

00:30:27.931 --> 00:30:28.539
hashes.

00:30:28.945 --> 00:30:31.998
Keeping the original loader avoids writing a
second

00:30:31.998 --> 00:30:35.139
interpretation of byte padding and
normalization.

00:30:35.557 --> 00:30:39.889
The surrounding code supplies validation,
reproducible commands,

00:30:40.130 --> 00:30:42.613
manifests, and independent output checks.

00:30:43.101 --> 00:30:45.774
The main entry point is repro dot p y.

00:30:46.179 --> 00:30:49.764
Its commands acquire or validate the pinned
source and data,

00:30:50.258 --> 00:30:53.577
resolve the original command line settings, and
launch training

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:54.565
subprocesses.

00:30:54.970 --> 00:30:58.859
A subprocess is a separately running program
invoked by the

00:30:58.859 --> 00:30:59.568
coordinator.

00:30:59.974 --> 00:31:04.078
The wrapper creates a fresh output directory and
a manifest

00:31:04.090 --> 00:31:08.372
recording identities, configuration,
environment, and status.

00:31:08.777 --> 00:31:11.450
A failed job should leave an informative record.

00:31:11.868 --> 00:31:15.706
It should not overwrite a prior successful
result and make the

00:31:15.719 --> 00:31:18.138
two runs impossible to distinguish.

00:31:18.543 --> 00:31:22.293
Shared preparation helpers are reused by the
other entry points.

00:31:22.768 --> 00:31:27.999
Evaluate dot p y loads a saved classifier
strictly and evaluates

00:31:28.012 --> 00:31:32.027
labeled test flows through the original loader
and evaluation

00:31:32.027 --> 00:31:32.344
engine.

00:31:32.749 --> 00:31:34.117
It does not train the model again.

00:31:34.522 --> 00:31:38.778
It also does not choose a checkpoint by
maximizing test accuracy.

00:31:39.196 --> 00:31:43.161
Its result is a set of metrics attached to the
evaluated model

00:31:43.174 --> 00:31:43.731
and data.

00:31:44.136 --> 00:31:47.315
If someone sends you a number without a
checkpoint identity or

00:31:47.569 --> 00:31:50.710
class mapping, you cannot establish that it
describes the

00:31:50.723 --> 00:31:52.040
intended classifier.

00:31:52.458 --> 00:31:56.575
This wrapper records those bindings so the
reported accuracy can

00:31:56.575 --> 00:31:58.639
be traced to a particular execution.

00:31:59.114 --> 00:32:03.406
Predict dot p y is for native flows whose true
categories are

00:32:03.406 --> 00:32:03.925
unknown.

00:32:04.305 --> 00:32:07.647
It adapts those records to the original loader&#x27;s
expected

00:32:07.647 --> 00:32:11.331
structure, removes target information before
prediction, and

00:32:11.331 --> 00:32:14.092
returns the classifier outputs with class names.

00:32:14.509 --> 00:32:18.016
This is useful for testing the inference
interface with already

00:32:18.092 --> 00:32:19.270
assembled flows.

00:32:19.688 --> 00:32:23.258
It is not a packet capture tool, and it cannot
reconstruct

00:32:23.271 --> 00:32:26.069
missing flow context from an arbitrary CSV.

00:32:26.474 --> 00:32:29.867
It does not calculate accuracy for unlabeled
records.

00:32:30.272 --> 00:32:33.855
That behavior prevents a common reporting error:
presenting

00:32:33.855 --> 00:32:37.932
confident looking predictions as evidence that
those predictions

00:32:37.944 --> 00:32:38.755
are correct.

00:32:39.222 --> 00:32:43.188
Replay dot p y records labeled model predictions
and

00:32:43.238 --> 00:32:46.241
independently recomputes metrics from the saved
outputs.

00:32:46.659 --> 00:32:50.080
It also builds the self contained browser
demonstration.

00:32:50.498 --> 00:32:54.172
The browser page displays the data already
written into it.

00:32:54.565 --> 00:32:57.973
It does not need CUDA or a model checkpoint to
replay those

00:32:57.973 --> 00:32:58.581
results.

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:02.661
This split is intentional: the numerical
experiment can be

00:33:02.661 --> 00:33:06.158
reviewed once and then explained on another
computer without

00:33:06.171 --> 00:33:07.070
rerunning training.

00:33:07.476 --> 00:33:11.061
The tradeoff is that the page is a recorded
demonstration.

00:33:11.467 --> 00:33:14.735
New customer flows require actual inference
through a checked

00:33:14.748 --> 00:33:17.561
runtime, not just a browser refresh.

00:33:18.035 --> 00:33:21.556
The configuration files are recipes and identity
checks.

00:33:21.974 --> 00:33:26.546
They contain settings, source pins, expected
asset hashes, and

00:33:26.558 --> 00:33:27.508
class metadata.

00:33:27.914 --> 00:33:32.017
The assets directory holds separately acquired
data and weights

00:33:32.030 --> 00:33:32.638
on your machine.

00:33:33.043 --> 00:33:37.311
The downloaded authors&#x27; source lives in the
local upstream slash

00:33:37.324 --> 00:33:39.401
Net Mamba Plus directory.

00:33:39.806 --> 00:33:43.555
Those external assets are not mirrored as public
repository

00:33:43.555 --> 00:33:44.303
contents.

00:33:44.708 --> 00:33:48.406
This distinction explains why cloning the
repository is enough

00:33:48.419 --> 00:33:51.686
for document checks but not enough for neural
training.

00:33:52.104 --> 00:33:56.335
The acquisition tools must obtain the additional
files, verify

00:33:56.347 --> 00:33:59.640
their identities, and preserve their unresolved
inherited

00:33:59.653 --> 00:34:01.793
redistribution and commercial terms.

00:34:02.268 --> 00:34:05.017
When auditing a run, start with its manifest.

00:34:05.422 --> 00:34:08.678
Then inspect the log and the saved metrics or
predictions.

00:34:09.096 --> 00:34:12.770
A configuration file proves what was requested,
not that the run

00:34:12.770 --> 00:34:13.365
completed.

00:34:13.745 --> 00:34:17.913
A model file proves saved state exists, not
which data produced

00:34:17.926 --> 00:34:18.078
it.

00:34:18.496 --> 00:34:21.929
A chart is a presentation of measurements, not
their origin.

00:34:22.347 --> 00:34:23.639
The records work together.

00:34:24.045 --> 00:34:27.465
Take ten seconds to decide which entry point you
would use for an

00:34:27.528 --> 00:34:30.607
unlabeled native flow, and which one you would
use to score a

00:34:30.620 --> 00:34:31.582
labeled test set.

00:34:31.988 --> 00:34:35.623
Then identify the file that records the run&#x27;s
provenance.

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:46.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Which scripts
handle unlabeled prediction and labeled
evaluation? Where is run provenance recorded?

00:34:46.101 --> 00:34:50.642
Use predict for unlabeled flows, evaluate for
labeled scoring,

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:52.728
and the manifest for run provenance.

00:34:53.133 --> 00:34:55.182
Now examine the recorded results.

00:34:55.587 --> 00:35:00.608
Seed zero correctly classified nine hundred
fifty of one thousand

00:35:00.608 --> 00:35:02.189
forty one test flows.

00:35:02.606 --> 00:35:05.173
Seed one got eight hundred seventy five correct.

00:35:05.578 --> 00:35:07.500
Seed two got eight hundred eighty one.

00:35:07.918 --> 00:35:12.155
Those correspond to ninety one point two six,
eighty four point

00:35:12.155 --> 00:35:16.619
zero five, and eighty four point six three
percent accuracy.

00:35:17.011 --> 00:35:18.466
All three results are retained.

00:35:18.870 --> 00:35:21.868
Selecting only the strongest seed would give a
misleading

00:35:21.868 --> 00:35:25.700
impression of this training procedure&#x27;s observed
variability.

00:35:26.168 --> 00:35:30.013
The average accuracy is eighty six point six
five percent.

00:35:30.418 --> 00:35:33.656
The sample standard deviation is four percentage
points.

00:35:34.074 --> 00:35:38.324
The mean summarizes these three runs; the
standard deviation

00:35:38.615 --> 00:35:39.943
summarizes their spread.

00:35:40.348 --> 00:35:43.232
It is not a confidence interval and does not
prove how

00:35:43.232 --> 00:35:45.585
performance will vary on a customer&#x27;s network.

00:35:45.989 --> 00:35:48.658
All three seeds used the same test split.

00:35:49.088 --> 00:35:53.022
One result has therefore been repeated under
different randomized

00:35:53.035 --> 00:35:57.184
training conditions, rather than evaluated on
three independently

00:35:57.197 --> 00:35:58.310
collected networks.

00:35:58.714 --> 00:36:01.611
Those are useful measurements, but they answer a
narrower

00:36:01.611 --> 00:36:03.989
question than real world generalization.

00:36:04.455 --> 00:36:08.616
Accuracy counts correct predictions across the
full test set.

00:36:09.021 --> 00:36:12.536
Precision asks how many predictions of a
category were right.

00:36:12.928 --> 00:36:17.114
Recall asks how many actual examples of that
category were found.

00:36:17.519 --> 00:36:20.074
F one combines precision and recall.

00:36:20.478 --> 00:36:24.829
Macro F one treats categories equally, while
weighted F one

00:36:24.829 --> 00:36:28.673
accounts for how many true examples each
category has.

00:36:29.078 --> 00:36:33.175
These metrics can disagree when class sizes
differ or errors

00:36:33.175 --> 00:36:34.997
concentrate in one category.

00:36:35.414 --> 00:36:39.221
The repository preserves independent metrics and
a confusion

00:36:39.221 --> 00:36:42.951
matrix so you can inspect the pattern, rather
than trust a single

00:36:42.951 --> 00:36:43.697
percentage.

00:36:44.155 --> 00:36:48.962
In the matrix, each row is a known category and
each column is

00:36:48.975 --> 00:36:49.949
the model&#x27;s prediction.

00:36:50.367 --> 00:36:52.226
Counts on the diagonal are correct.

00:36:52.644 --> 00:36:56.793
Off diagonal counts show which categories were
confused.

00:36:57.185 --> 00:37:01.132
Our test set has two hundred examples for each
category except

00:37:01.183 --> 00:37:02.777
Other, which has forty one.

00:37:03.182 --> 00:37:06.332
That support difference matters when comparing
percentages.

00:37:06.749 --> 00:37:10.291
The matrix is computed from saved predictions,
so another

00:37:10.304 --> 00:37:11.594
reviewer can recompute it.

00:37:11.999 --> 00:37:15.313
A large diagonal is encouraging, but the
mistakes remain

00:37:15.326 --> 00:37:19.817
important: an operational system must understand
the cost of both

00:37:19.817 --> 00:37:23.245
missed attacks and incorrect alerts on benign
activity.

00:37:23.734 --> 00:37:28.134
The final native evaluation, strict reloaded
evaluation, and

00:37:28.134 --> 00:37:30.966
recorded replay agree for the three seeds.

00:37:31.358 --> 00:37:35.833
A later fresh environment on the same G B ten
hardware reproduced

00:37:36.048 --> 00:37:40.081
all one thousand forty one seed zero predictions
and all six

00:37:40.081 --> 00:37:43.583
thousand two hundred forty six saved logits
exactly.

00:37:44.000 --> 00:37:47.034
That is strong evidence that the checked
environment can

00:37:47.034 --> 00:37:50.132
reexecute the frozen classifier result.

00:37:50.536 --> 00:37:53.520
It is not another training run or an independent
dataset

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:54.253
evaluation.

00:37:54.658 --> 00:37:58.564
Smaller differences in an earlier limited
comparison remain

00:37:58.577 --> 00:37:59.765
recorded separately.

00:38:00.157 --> 00:38:03.899
Each check retains its own inputs, environment,
and scope.

00:38:04.381 --> 00:38:07.176
The paper comparison is higher than our measured
result.

00:38:07.593 --> 00:38:11.325
Possible contributing factors include runtime
differences,

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:15.752
training settings, extraction details, and
checkpoint history,

00:38:16.207 --> 00:38:18.572
but we have not isolated their effects.

00:38:18.977 --> 00:38:22.810
Naming plausible causes is not the same as
testing them.

00:38:23.202 --> 00:38:27.060
A defensible follow up would change one relevant
factor under a

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:30.019
controlled protocol and retain every outcome.

00:38:30.437 --> 00:38:33.902
It would also address data independence and
record exact

00:38:33.902 --> 00:38:34.864
preprocessing.

00:38:35.294 --> 00:38:39.329
For now the accurate statement is that the model
executes and

00:38:39.329 --> 00:38:42.769
trains, while the paper&#x27;s headline accuracy has
not been

00:38:42.769 --> 00:38:44.945
reproduced under our measured conditions.

00:38:45.434 --> 00:38:48.519
A customer can reasonably ask what confidence
these checks

00:38:48.519 --> 00:38:49.037
provide.

00:38:49.454 --> 00:38:51.692
Answer with the activity actually tested.

00:38:52.147 --> 00:38:55.876
Three complete training runs produced recorded
classifiers.

00:38:56.293 --> 00:38:58.960
Strict inference and independent metrics agree.

00:38:59.427 --> 00:39:02.524
Fresh runtime checks reproduce the frozen
result.

00:39:02.929 --> 00:39:06.317
The data and runtime limitations remain
explicit.

00:39:06.734 --> 00:39:09.413
None of those statements requires pretending
that the paper

00:39:09.426 --> 00:39:13.029
number was matched or that an operational
network was protected.

00:39:13.446 --> 00:39:17.453
Take ten seconds to practice explaining the
average accuracy and

00:39:17.453 --> 00:39:20.828
the four percentage point spread, while
mentioning that all seeds

00:39:20.828 --> 00:39:22.623
used the same fixed test set.

00:39:23.000 --> 00:39:33.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Explain the mean
and spread without claiming customer-network
validation.

00:39:33.101 --> 00:39:36.071
Now use the browser replay to make the result
concrete.

00:39:36.488 --> 00:39:39.092
The page can be opened on a presentation machine
without

00:39:39.105 --> 00:39:42.492
installing CUDA, Python, or a checkpoint.

00:39:42.896 --> 00:39:46.802
It displays seed zero&#x27;s saved predictions and
their known labels.

00:39:47.219 --> 00:39:51.087
Its controls let you inspect the recorded
sequence and errors.

00:39:51.516 --> 00:39:53.779
The screenshot comes from the actual working
page.

00:39:54.196 --> 00:39:57.634
This is a good customer demonstration because
the evidence is

00:39:57.634 --> 00:40:01.033
available offline and can be inspected
consistently.

00:40:01.438 --> 00:40:06.013
Say recorded replay before presenting it, so a
moving table is

00:40:06.013 --> 00:40:08.503
not mistaken for a live packet feed.

00:40:08.968 --> 00:40:12.100
Seed zero was declared as the demonstration
model.

00:40:12.517 --> 00:40:15.056
The other two results remain published beside
it.

00:40:15.435 --> 00:40:20.147
A demo often needs one fixed model and a
manageable story, but

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:22.459
that choice must not hide variability.

00:40:22.876 --> 00:40:27.031
Also, the number of predictions assigned to
attack categories is

00:40:27.044 --> 00:40:29.621
not the number of operational security alerts.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.676
A real alert policy might combine repeated
observations,

00:40:34.042 --> 00:40:37.567
thresholds, exceptions, and analyst context.

00:40:37.971 --> 00:40:40.965
Those policy components have not been
implemented here.

00:40:41.369 --> 00:40:45.563
The recorded page lets you explain
classification behavior; it

00:40:45.563 --> 00:40:48.556
does not establish an incident response
workflow.

00:40:49.022 --> 00:40:52.673
Look at the saved prediction with JSON index six
hundred thirty

00:40:52.686 --> 00:40:53.179
five.

00:40:53.583 --> 00:40:57.487
The browser numbers rows from one, so it
displays that record as

00:40:57.500 --> 00:40:59.168
zero six three six.

00:40:59.585 --> 00:41:02.870
The highest score belongs to R T S P brute
force.

00:41:03.287 --> 00:41:07.014
Its normalized display score is eighty four
point four three

00:41:07.014 --> 00:41:07.570
percent.

00:41:07.987 --> 00:41:11.689
Before looking at the true label, notice how
confident that

00:41:11.689 --> 00:41:13.003
display can appear.

00:41:13.408 --> 00:41:17.034
Softmax converts six logits into scores that add
to one.

00:41:17.463 --> 00:41:20.989
That arithmetic does not establish that the top
score is a

00:41:20.989 --> 00:41:23.212
calibrated probability of an attack.

00:41:23.701 --> 00:41:25.193
The known label is Other.

00:41:25.610 --> 00:41:26.760
This prediction is wrong.

00:41:27.164 --> 00:41:30.880
That single record gives you a useful way to
explain why a high

00:41:30.893 --> 00:41:34.862
display score is not sufficient evidence to
block traffic.

00:41:35.279 --> 00:41:38.855
The model must assign one of its known
categories, even when a

00:41:38.855 --> 00:41:43.153
flow is ambiguous or unlike its training
examples.

00:41:43.583 --> 00:41:47.475
The project has not validated an unknown attack
detector or a

00:41:47.488 --> 00:41:49.270
confidence calibration procedure.

00:41:49.662 --> 00:41:53.618
Showing a real error makes the demonstration
more informative: it

00:41:53.631 --> 00:41:57.852
reveals the gap between a classifier&#x27;s output
and an operational

00:41:57.852 --> 00:41:58.813
security decision.

00:41:59.301 --> 00:42:02.168
If you want new predictions, use the prediction
command with

00:42:02.181 --> 00:42:05.048
already assembled native flows and a checked
classifier

00:42:05.061 --> 00:42:05.642
checkpoint.

00:42:06.058 --> 00:42:09.014
The input path identifies your flow JSON.

00:42:09.443 --> 00:42:11.692
The checkpoint path identifies the model.

00:42:12.108 --> 00:42:15.367
The output path must be fresh so that provenance
stays

00:42:15.493 --> 00:42:16.479
unambiguous.

00:42:16.908 --> 00:42:20.963
The command returns class names, logits, and
display scores.

00:42:21.379 --> 00:42:25.181
It will not invent accuracy when no correct
labels are supplied.

00:42:25.585 --> 00:42:29.160
This interface is a useful component for a
future service, but

00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:33.303
capture, flow creation, and customer data
validation still need

00:42:33.303 --> 00:42:34.161
to be built around it.

00:42:34.634 --> 00:42:38.197
A simple future prototype would capture packets,
group them into

00:42:38.209 --> 00:42:42.858
flows, produce the exact expected feature
representation, invoke

00:42:42.858 --> 00:42:45.334
the classifier, and log outputs for review.

00:42:45.751 --> 00:42:49.212
Initially, it could run in observation mode
while comparing

00:42:49.212 --> 00:42:51.789
predictions with independently established
labels.

00:42:52.205 --> 00:42:55.907
The original extraction code has unresolved
dependencies and path

00:42:55.907 --> 00:42:59.545
assumptions, so that capture to feature route is
not currently

00:42:59.545 --> 00:43:01.187
established by this repository.

00:43:01.604 --> 00:43:05.507
Do not insert a box labeled preprocessing into a
diagram and

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:07.553
assume the engineering is complete.

00:43:07.957 --> 00:43:12.328
Its grouping, timing, byte extraction, and
truncation rules are

00:43:12.341 --> 00:43:14.501
part of the model&#x27;s effective specification.

00:43:14.968 --> 00:43:18.605
To hand this demo to another presenter, show the
saved result,

00:43:19.035 --> 00:43:23.203
reveal the incorrect example, and explain the
missing operational

00:43:23.203 --> 00:43:23.885
stages.

00:43:24.302 --> 00:43:27.863
Keep the native inference command available for
an engineer who

00:43:27.863 --> 00:43:30.339
wants to run new compatible flows.

00:43:30.756 --> 00:43:34.709
Keep the offline replay available for a meeting
with unreliable

00:43:34.722 --> 00:43:35.202
internet.

00:43:35.581 --> 00:43:38.688
Neither route needs a chatbot to classify
traffic.

00:43:39.092 --> 00:43:43.828
Take ten seconds to explain why the eighty four
point four three

00:43:43.828 --> 00:43:47.491
percent score was not enough to trust this
particular prediction,

00:43:47.971 --> 00:43:50.636
even though the score calculation itself was
valid.

00:43:51.000 --> 00:44:01.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Why was the 84.43%
display score insufficient to trust this
prediction?

00:44:01.101 --> 00:44:04.340
The score was uncalibrated and the known label
showed an error.

00:44:04.732 --> 00:44:06.921
Next, choose the setup you actually need.

00:44:07.376 --> 00:44:11.399
For a presentation, use the video, documents,
and recorded

00:44:11.399 --> 00:44:12.222
browser demo.

00:44:12.639 --> 00:44:15.751
For portable evidence checks, use Git and
Python.

00:44:16.194 --> 00:44:20.255
For new neural training or inference, use the
checked Linux CUDA

00:44:20.255 --> 00:44:22.962
environment and separately acquired assets.

00:44:23.367 --> 00:44:25.303
These routes have different requirements.

00:44:25.708 --> 00:44:28.972
A laptop can display the presentation without
supporting the

00:44:28.972 --> 00:44:30.401
model&#x27;s CUDA extensions.

00:44:30.819 --> 00:44:34.753
This separation makes the project usable on
other systems while

00:44:34.766 --> 00:44:38.194
keeping the model execution claims tied to
hardware that was

00:44:38.207 --> 00:44:39.131
actually tested.

00:44:39.601 --> 00:44:42.941
For the portable route, clone the repository,
enter its

00:44:42.941 --> 00:44:45.105
directory, and run the commands shown.

00:44:45.523 --> 00:44:48.977
Use Python three if that is your system&#x27;s
executable name.

00:44:49.394 --> 00:44:53.000
The unit tests and the package and video source
checks use the

00:44:53.000 --> 00:44:55.885
standard library without downloading research
data.

00:44:56.302 --> 00:45:00.528
A successful suite ends with O K, and the
verifiers exit

00:45:00.541 --> 00:45:01.338
successfully.

00:45:01.743 --> 00:45:05.577
These checks protect important behavior such as
input rejection,

00:45:05.994 --> 00:45:09.081
file identity, and consistent published
artifacts.

00:45:09.499 --> 00:45:12.574
They are deliberately different from a GPU
training test.

00:45:12.978 --> 00:45:16.597
Read the workflow record for the exact test
count and commit that

00:45:16.610 --> 00:45:17.192
passed.

00:45:17.668 --> 00:45:20.731
The automated workflow runs the portable checks
on Linux and

00:45:20.731 --> 00:45:25.541
Windows with x eighty six processors, and macOS
on ARM, using

00:45:25.541 --> 00:45:28.464
Python three point ten and three point twelve.

00:45:28.895 --> 00:45:30.528
That makes six combinations.

00:45:30.945 --> 00:45:35.540
Earlier failures exposed text encoding, newline,
and temporary

00:45:35.540 --> 00:45:36.565
directory issues.

00:45:36.970 --> 00:45:40.856
Those were corrected and retained as regression
tests rather than

00:45:40.856 --> 00:45:42.830
removing platforms from the matrix.

00:45:43.223 --> 00:45:46.868
A green workflow means those particular checks
passed on the

00:45:46.868 --> 00:45:47.691
recorded commit.

00:45:48.108 --> 00:45:52.627
It does not establish native Windows or macOS
execution of the

00:45:52.627 --> 00:45:53.804
original neural model.

00:45:54.222 --> 00:45:56.918
The support matrix keeps those boundaries
separate.

00:45:57.401 --> 00:46:00.854
The GPU route uses a specific environment
profile.

00:46:01.272 --> 00:46:05.231
It includes Python three point twelve, Torch two
point nine point

00:46:05.231 --> 00:46:09.266
one with CUDA thirteen, and pinned native
extensions for Mamba

00:46:09.569 --> 00:46:10.733
and causal convolution.

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:14.426
These extensions contain compiled GPU code.

00:46:14.818 --> 00:46:19.182
A compatible Python package name alone is not
enough: the driver,

00:46:19.385 --> 00:46:23.786
compiler, selected GPU architecture, and
installed binaries must

00:46:23.786 --> 00:46:24.469
work together.

00:46:24.887 --> 00:46:27.872
The current support guide provides the full
commands.

00:46:28.277 --> 00:46:32.893
The generalized builder uses disposable source
copies, checks the

00:46:32.893 --> 00:46:36.486
selected target, and records actual compiler
flags.

00:46:36.878 --> 00:46:39.698
It preserves the original model&#x27;s source
identity.

00:46:40.168 --> 00:46:43.937
After building, run the numerical checker and
the complete model

00:46:43.950 --> 00:46:44.494
checker.

00:46:44.898 --> 00:46:48.908
The numerical checker compares selected GPU
operations with

00:46:48.920 --> 00:46:50.248
reference computations.

00:46:50.653 --> 00:46:54.056
The complete model checker processes six
synthetic fixtures

00:46:54.233 --> 00:46:58.862
through all four blocks, performs an optimizer
update, saves and

00:46:58.862 --> 00:47:02.100
strictly reloads the state, and checks
inference.

00:47:02.505 --> 00:47:06.059
It can bind installed extension hashes to the
completed build

00:47:06.059 --> 00:47:06.577
report.

00:47:06.969 --> 00:47:10.018
This catches failures that a successful import
would miss.

00:47:10.435 --> 00:47:14.116
Synthetic fixtures prove functionality, not
accuracy.

00:47:14.520 --> 00:47:18.214
Only after those gates pass should you acquire
and validate the

00:47:18.214 --> 00:47:21.566
real flow assets and start a new measured
experiment.

00:47:22.035 --> 00:47:25.804
The original model has measured execution on G B
ten.

00:47:26.222 --> 00:47:29.637
A fresh environment on that same hardware also
passed the

00:47:29.650 --> 00:47:31.952
generalized build and model checks.

00:47:32.357 --> 00:47:36.127
Other physical NVIDIA GPUs still need their own
validation.

00:47:36.532 --> 00:47:40.795
There is no complete CPU model fallback, Apple
graphics backend,

00:47:41.250 --> 00:47:43.337
or validated N P U implementation.

00:47:43.742 --> 00:47:47.575
Some reference formulas exist, but normal
imports and active

00:47:47.575 --> 00:47:51.573
execution still depend on CUDA and fused
operations.

00:47:51.977 --> 00:47:56.380
If a setup fails, inspect the fresh build report
and logs.

00:47:56.759 --> 00:48:00.504
Do not turn off checks until it appears green;
determine which

00:48:00.504 --> 00:48:03.362
prerequisite or computation actually failed.

00:48:03.822 --> 00:48:07.162
You can now explain setup without promising that
every machine

00:48:07.174 --> 00:48:08.566
runs every layer.

00:48:08.983 --> 00:48:10.666
Use the simplest route for the job.

00:48:11.083 --> 00:48:13.600
Preserve output directories and their manifests.

00:48:14.005 --> 00:48:17.762
Read failures before retrying with a corrected
configuration.

00:48:18.180 --> 00:48:21.785
When someone asks whether it works elsewhere,
specify whether

00:48:21.798 --> 00:48:26.225
they mean opening the materials, rechecking
evidence, or running

00:48:26.238 --> 00:48:26.997
the neural model.

00:48:27.402 --> 00:48:31.513
Take ten seconds to choose the correct route for
a colleague who

00:48:31.589 --> 00:48:34.992
only needs to present the demo on a Windows
laptop, and the

00:48:34.992 --> 00:48:38.623
separate route for an engineer who wants new GPU
predictions.

00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:49.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Which setup is
enough to present, and which setup is needed for
new model predictions?

00:48:49.089 --> 00:48:52.605
A presenter needs the viewing route; new neural
predictions

00:48:52.605 --> 00:48:54.604
require the checked GPU route.

00:48:54.996 --> 00:48:57.298
Now tour the rest of the repository.

00:48:57.716 --> 00:49:02.004
The file guide is the complete inventory, with
one entry for

00:49:02.193 --> 00:49:03.307
every tracked file.

00:49:03.711 --> 00:49:07.215
This chapter explains how the areas fit together
so that the

00:49:07.228 --> 00:49:12.085
inventory is useful instead of becoming a wall
of unfamiliar

00:49:12.085 --> 00:49:12.655
names.

00:49:13.059 --> 00:49:14.679
Tracked means included in Git.

00:49:15.083 --> 00:49:19.776
Locally downloaded source, raw data,
checkpoints, environments,

00:49:19.953 --> 00:49:21.585
and temporary runs are separate.

00:49:21.977 --> 00:49:25.481
A GitHub release attachment is another separate
object: it

00:49:25.481 --> 00:49:29.048
belongs to its tagged release, not automatically
to the current

00:49:29.048 --> 00:49:29.795
working tree.

00:49:30.268 --> 00:49:33.707
At the root, the read me is the front door and
the four Python

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:35.553
entry points coordinate execution.

00:49:35.971 --> 00:49:39.954
Configs contains the source based fine tuning
recipe, the short

00:49:39.954 --> 00:49:42.748
pretraining recipe, and the asset inventory.

00:49:43.153 --> 00:49:46.592
Requirements separates packages used for
different activities,

00:49:46.832 --> 00:49:50.992
such as GPU execution, browser checks, and video
production.

00:49:51.410 --> 00:49:56.101
Dot git ignore keeps local outputs and sensitive
settings out of

00:49:56.101 --> 00:49:56.910
normal tracking.

00:49:57.315 --> 00:50:01.045
Dot git attributes preserves the exact bytes of
recorded

00:50:01.045 --> 00:50:04.383
documents and configurations across platforms.

00:50:04.775 --> 00:50:08.720
Neither file establishes data rights or
scientific correctness;

00:50:09.125 --> 00:50:11.527
they support reliable repository handling.

00:50:12.001 --> 00:50:14.708
The tools directory groups supporting programs.

00:50:15.177 --> 00:50:18.694
Fetch assets obtains verified external files.

00:50:19.111 --> 00:50:22.577
Profile C S Vs produces the data profiles.

00:50:22.995 --> 00:50:25.348
Capture runtime records the environment.

00:50:25.765 --> 00:50:29.105
The CUDA builders compile the pinned extensions,
while the

00:50:29.118 --> 00:50:31.787
numerical and model checkers exercise them.

00:50:32.217 --> 00:50:35.329
Benchmark measures model execution timing.

00:50:35.722 --> 00:50:38.037
Probe export tests graph capture.

00:50:38.404 --> 00:50:40.959
Review experiments collects run results.

00:50:41.377 --> 00:50:45.311
Publish evidence copies reviewed records into
the package; its

00:50:45.311 --> 00:50:48.879
name does not mean arbitrary files are
automatically sent to

00:50:48.879 --> 00:50:49.397
GitHub.

00:50:49.815 --> 00:50:54.331
Each tool&#x27;s entry in the file guide states its
input, output, and

00:50:54.331 --> 00:50:55.115
when you would use it.

00:50:55.589 --> 00:50:58.840
Other tools build the customer package, the
plain language

00:50:58.840 --> 00:51:02.471
learning guide, replay outputs, and teaching
materials.

00:51:02.876 --> 00:51:06.786
The video pipeline separates the lesson source,
speech rendering,

00:51:07.013 --> 00:51:09.442
visual assembly, and browser page.

00:51:09.860 --> 00:51:12.947
Codex authors the narration and explanations.

00:51:13.352 --> 00:51:17.072
Microsoft&#x27;s Andrew neural voice converts that
text into sound;

00:51:17.540 --> 00:51:20.513
the speech service does not research or write
the lesson.

00:51:20.930 --> 00:51:25.220
Verification tools compare identities and
inspect encoded media.

00:51:25.637 --> 00:51:29.142
The archived interactive course builders remain
for reference

00:51:29.154 --> 00:51:33.165
continuity, but the retired public course route
is excluded from

00:51:33.165 --> 00:51:33.747
deployment.

00:51:34.152 --> 00:51:36.859
The one active video is the current teaching
path.

00:51:37.335 --> 00:51:41.243
Tests contains controlled cases for the
wrappers, acquisition,

00:51:41.647 --> 00:51:45.100
portability, runtime checking, and presentation
tooling.

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:49.868
The workflow files run portable checks and
publish the selected

00:51:49.868 --> 00:51:50.791
browser material.

00:51:51.209 --> 00:51:54.990
Docs contains foundational explanations and the
customer package.

00:51:55.445 --> 00:51:59.556
Figures are reusable charts; evidence contains
original

00:51:59.556 --> 00:52:03.780
experiment records; audit evidence contains
later rechecks.

00:52:04.185 --> 00:52:07.663
Research records preserve plans and council
outcomes.

00:52:08.067 --> 00:52:11.166
A council discussion can improve a design, but
it is not an

00:52:11.280 --> 00:52:12.937
executed GPU test.

00:52:13.341 --> 00:52:17.730
An unratified review remains unratified even
when ordinary

00:52:17.730 --> 00:52:20.525
implementation later fixes a technical problem.

00:52:20.930 --> 00:52:24.256
Read the status and evidence scope of each
record.

00:52:24.735 --> 00:52:29.175
The original customer release contains a
briefing PDF, editable

00:52:29.188 --> 00:52:33.528
PowerPoint, slide PDF, offline package, and
supporting

00:52:33.540 --> 00:52:36.020
presentation materials at its recorded revision.

00:52:36.438 --> 00:52:38.892
It remains a frozen experiment package.

00:52:39.310 --> 00:52:43.371
The new course has its own versioned video,
transcript, captions,

00:52:43.586 --> 00:52:44.915
and companion materials.

00:52:45.307 --> 00:52:47.584
A transcript is the narration in text.

00:52:47.989 --> 00:52:51.102
Caption files add time boundaries for video
players.

00:52:51.507 --> 00:52:55.846
Chapter metadata supports navigation, and the
media manifest

00:52:55.859 --> 00:52:57.997
records hashes and provenance.

00:52:58.415 --> 00:53:01.881
Use the current support matrix for later
portability work.

00:53:02.299 --> 00:53:06.348
Do not assume the original release ZIP silently
changes whenever

00:53:06.348 --> 00:53:08.600
the repository gains a new file.

00:53:09.106 --> 00:53:12.823
For a new colleague, begin with the read me and
this course, then

00:53:12.823 --> 00:53:15.857
use the file guide to explore a specific task.

00:53:16.262 --> 00:53:19.903
Before running commands, read the current
support matrix and the

00:53:19.903 --> 00:53:20.447
runbook.

00:53:20.864 --> 00:53:24.037
Before presenting a result, trace it from the
results document to

00:53:24.050 --> 00:53:27.135
its manifest, log, and predictions.

00:53:27.527 --> 00:53:30.498
You do not need to memorize every audit JSON
file.

00:53:30.915 --> 00:53:33.317
You need to know which question it answers and
how it was

00:53:33.317 --> 00:53:33.924
produced.

00:53:34.341 --> 00:53:37.793
Take ten seconds to choose the file you would
open to check a

00:53:37.793 --> 00:53:41.864
platform claim, and the record you would open to
verify an actual

00:53:41.864 --> 00:53:42.623
training result.

00:53:43.000 --> 00:53:53.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Where would you
verify a platform claim and an actual training
result?

00:53:53.101 --> 00:53:56.849
Use the support matrix for platform claims and
the run records

00:53:56.849 --> 00:53:57.951
for training evidence.

00:53:58.432 --> 00:54:00.420
Finally, consider hardware deployment.

00:54:00.825 --> 00:54:04.852
The model only benchmark measured a median batch
one latency of

00:54:04.864 --> 00:54:08.473
about zero point eight eight milliseconds on G B
ten.

00:54:08.878 --> 00:54:12.271
It used twenty warmup iterations and one hundred
measured

00:54:12.271 --> 00:54:13.107
repetitions.

00:54:13.500 --> 00:54:17.501
That timing excludes packet capture, waiting for
enough packets,

00:54:17.665 --> 00:54:21.666
preprocessing, transfers, model loading, and
alert handling.

00:54:22.059 --> 00:54:25.123
A fast classifier can still be part of a slow
pipeline.

00:54:25.503 --> 00:54:29.542
End to end latency must be measured across the
complete path a

00:54:29.555 --> 00:54:31.429
real observation follows.

00:54:31.876 --> 00:54:35.635
A network interface card moves traffic into and
out of a machine.

00:54:36.040 --> 00:54:39.319
A Smart N I C can take on additional packet
handling or

00:54:39.319 --> 00:54:40.091
computation.

00:54:40.496 --> 00:54:44.976
An N P U accelerates neural operations that its
runtime supports.

00:54:45.381 --> 00:54:49.179
A possible future division places capture and
some flow handling

00:54:49.191 --> 00:54:52.457
near the network interface, with a host
coordinating buffers,

00:54:52.697 --> 00:54:55.583
features, inference, and alert policy.

00:54:55.988 --> 00:54:57.735
This is an architectural proposal.

00:54:58.140 --> 00:55:02.950
Our repository has not executed Net Mamba Plus
on a Smart N I C

00:55:03.077 --> 00:55:07.342
or N P U, and it does not establish a direct
memory transfer path

00:55:07.342 --> 00:55:08.291
on the measured hardware.

00:55:08.743 --> 00:55:12.971
A model only checkpoint export saves parameter
tensors without

00:55:12.984 --> 00:55:14.326
the training optimizer.

00:55:14.718 --> 00:55:18.681
A graph export tries to represent the
computation for another

00:55:18.694 --> 00:55:19.744
execution system.

00:55:20.162 --> 00:55:21.416
These are different operations.

00:55:21.821 --> 00:55:25.277
Our strict graph capture probe failed at the
custom causal

00:55:25.277 --> 00:55:29.873
convolution CUDA operator, while ordinary eager
GPU inference

00:55:29.873 --> 00:55:30.379
works.

00:55:30.797 --> 00:55:34.494
Copying the weight file to an N P U therefore
does not solve

00:55:34.494 --> 00:55:35.076
deployment.

00:55:35.481 --> 00:55:39.305
A port needs supported operators or equivalent
implementations,

00:55:39.520 --> 00:55:42.824
compatible layouts, controlled precision, and
numerical

00:55:42.837 --> 00:55:44.736
validation against the known outputs.

00:55:45.141 --> 00:55:48.965
The recorded failure is useful because it
identifies a concrete

00:55:49.066 --> 00:55:52.193
obstacle rather than a vague future
optimization.

00:55:52.668 --> 00:55:56.339
After implementing a target backend, first
compare its full model

00:55:56.339 --> 00:55:57.959
outputs with the checked reference.

00:55:58.364 --> 00:56:01.920
Where training is part of the target, compare
gradients as well.

00:56:02.338 --> 00:56:06.237
If reducing numerical precision through
quantization, measure the

00:56:06.237 --> 00:56:10.603
effect on the labeled task instead of assuming
the same accuracy.

00:56:10.996 --> 00:56:15.565
Then measure throughput, tail latency, memory,
buffering, and

00:56:15.565 --> 00:56:18.160
packet drops under an actual traffic path.

00:56:18.552 --> 00:56:23.008
A compiler success message proves a build stage,
not useful

00:56:23.020 --> 00:56:24.084
security behavior.

00:56:24.489 --> 00:56:28.134
Only a complete measured pipeline can support a
deployment claim,

00:56:28.590 --> 00:56:32.260
and none of those N P U or Smart N I C
measurements exists

00:56:32.260 --> 00:56:32.830
here yet.

00:56:33.301 --> 00:56:35.036
The remaining work is specific.

00:56:35.429 --> 00:56:39.494
We have not matched the paper&#x27;s accuracy or
repeated its full

00:56:39.494 --> 00:56:40.178
pretraining.

00:56:40.583 --> 00:56:43.952
We have not established the live capture and
extraction path,

00:56:44.205 --> 00:56:47.498
capture independent customer validation,
calibrated attack

00:56:47.498 --> 00:56:51.601
confidence, unknown attack detection, or an N P
U deployment.

00:56:52.006 --> 00:56:55.906
Other physical GPU configurations still need
their own checks.

00:56:56.312 --> 00:56:59.959
Data and inherited asset terms remain
unresolved.

00:57:00.377 --> 00:57:03.454
Those items should stay beside the positive
evidence.

00:57:03.872 --> 00:57:07.734
They define the next engineering and research
milestones rather

00:57:07.747 --> 00:57:11.559
than being hidden behind a claim that everything
is complete.

00:57:12.035 --> 00:57:14.857
Now explain the project aloud in your own words.

00:57:15.262 --> 00:57:18.844
Start with the paper&#x27;s idea, distinguish the
uploaded packet

00:57:18.844 --> 00:57:22.692
files from the training flows, and follow inputs
through training

00:57:22.856 --> 00:57:23.666
and inference.

00:57:24.084 --> 00:57:26.615
Give the measured result and one real error.

00:57:26.995 --> 00:57:30.298
Explain what the repository adds and which setup
your listener

00:57:30.311 --> 00:57:30.842
needs.

00:57:31.285 --> 00:57:33.235
Finish with the remaining deployment work.

00:57:33.652 --> 00:57:36.639
You have ninety seconds, and you can pause
longer.

00:57:37.057 --> 00:57:41.246
The transcript, slides, and every file guide
remain available

00:57:41.246 --> 00:57:41.752
afterward.

00:57:42.170 --> 00:57:46.372
Your strongest presentation is one in which each
claim leads to

00:57:46.372 --> 00:57:50.663
evidence and each limitation leads to a clear
next test.

00:57:51.033 --> 00:59:21.033
[Practice pause: 90 seconds] Explain the data
choice, tested result, repository and remaining
deployment work.

00:59:21.147 --> 00:59:22.578
Here is a concise handoff.

00:59:22.970 --> 00:59:26.515
We built a checked execution and evidence layer
around the

00:59:26.528 --> 00:59:29.731
authors&#x27; original Net Mamba Plus model.

00:59:30.136 --> 00:59:34.492
We profiled the two uploaded packet CSVs and
established why they

00:59:34.504 --> 00:59:36.467
were not compatible flow inputs.

00:59:36.859 --> 00:59:40.784
Using the authors&#x27; native flow data, three full
training runs

00:59:40.898 --> 00:59:44.785
averaged eighty six point six five percent test
accuracy.

00:59:45.190 --> 00:59:49.761
The repository includes strict inference,
independent results, a

00:59:49.862 --> 00:59:53.470
recorded demo, setup checks, and teaching
materials.

00:59:53.888 --> 00:59:57.509
The paper number and a live deployed intrusion
detection system

00:59:57.699 --> 00:59:59.623
remain unproven by this work.
